From 8ac3373d3548a88e8de979e887774b457aedd03e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: INTEL-CAMERA WIP <wip@localhost>
Date: Tue, 11 Aug 2026 21:36:44 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 2/5] BOZZA media: i2c: Add GC5035 image sensor driver

NON INVIABILE: attribuzione della patch Intel non sciolta, manca il
Signed-off-by. Vedi patches/wip/README.md nel progetto.

PROVATO SU HARDWARE il 2026-08-11 su CHUWI Hi10 X1: chip ID 0x5035
letto, cattura a 2592x1944, test pattern pixel-esatto, guadagno
analogico 16x chiesti e 15,7x misurati, v4l2-compliance 45/46.

Driver for the GalaxyCore GC5035, structured after gc05a2.c with the
x86/ACPI adaptations used by ov2740.c and t4ka3.c: ACPI match table,
optional reset/powerdown GPIOs, devm_v4l2_sensor_clk_get(), 8-bit CCI
regmap with page banking via register 0xfe, and
enable_streams/disable_streams instead of the deprecated s_stream.

The register tables come from the Intel patch for Alder Lake-M and set
the PLL up once, so the CSI-2 link frequency is a fixed multiple of the
external clock. It is therefore derived from the clock at probe time
rather than hardcoded, which keeps the driver correct on a platform
clocking the part differently.

The multiplier is measured rather than taken from the vendor code,
because the vendor code is wrong: it declares 438 MHz, which is a whole
multiple of no plausible external clock. With the 19.2 MHz an IPU6
machine provides, the frame rate settles at 28.81 fps against the 29.88
the declared value predicts, which works out at 422.4 MHz, that is
19.2 MHz * 22 to within 0.02%. The ratio is unchanged at three
different VBLANK settings, so what is wrong is the constant and not the
timing model.

Target hardware: CHUWI Hi10 X1 (Intel N100, Alder Lake-N, IPU6),
ACPI HID GCTI5035 at \_SB.PC00.LNK1.
